      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The National Training School for Women and Girls, founded by Nannie Helen Burroughs in 1909, was a pioneering boarding school for African‑American girls.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.deanwoodcitizens.org/history-of-dca",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Strand Theater opened November 3, 1928 and served as a key African‑American entertainment venue for over four decades until 1959.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.deanwoodcitizens.org/history-of-dca",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1968, after Martin Luther King Jr.’s assassination, Deanwood’s small black and Jewish‑owned businesses faced upheaval, yet continued to thrive.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://dchistory.libguides.com/c.php?g=1246926&p=9124575",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The National Training School building was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1991, cementing its historic significance.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
